  "nl_text": "The database schema named rschema_0167 is used to store information about natural disasters, including their data, source, and format. It consists of five tables: natural_disaster_data, region_information, department, user, and region_and_department.\nThe natural_disaster_data table contains information about the data itself, including its ID, type, source, upload time, content, format, and layer type. The region_information table contains information about the regions affected by the disasters, including their ID, name, administrative division, latitude and longitude, area, and disaster risk level. The department table contains information about the departments responsible for handling the disasters, including their ID, name, person-in-charge, contact information, functional scope, and department ID. The user table contains information about the users who have access to the system, including their ID, username, role, contact information, permissions, and department ID. The region_and_department table contains information about the division of regions and departments, including their ID, department ID, and division scope.\nThe primary keys for each table are data_id, region_id, department_id, user_id, and department_id. The foreign keys for each table are region_id, department_id, and department_id. The update and delete rules for each foreign key are CASCADE and RESTRICT, respectively. The indexes for each table are data_id, region_id, department_id, user_id, and department_id.\nIn summary, the rschema_0167 database schema is used to store information about natural disasters, including their data, source, and format, as well as the regions and departments affected by the disasters, the departments responsible for handling the disasters, and the users who have access to the system. The schema is organized into five tables, each with its own primary and foreign keys, and each with its own update and delete rules.",
  "gold_question": "The Jinan City Natural Disaster Comprehensive Risk Data Management System is based on a Browser/Server (B/S) architecture. The system aims to integrate and manage the results of the natural disaster risk survey in Jinan City, achieving efficient data management and multi-level sharing. The main functional requirements of the system include:\n1.Natural Disaster Data Management: The system will record relevant data related to natural disasters, such as Data ID, Data Type, Data Source, Upload Time, Data Content, Data Format, and Layer Type, to analyze natural disaster risks.\n2.Regional Data Management: Each region has Attributes like Region Name, Administrative Division, Latitude and Longitude, Area, and Disaster Risk Level, and each region may contain multiple natural disaster data.\n3.Department Data Management: Each department records Attributes such as Department Name, Person-in-Charge, Contact Information, and Functional Scope. Each region can have multiple departments, and each department can manage multiple regions.\n4.User Management: Each user has a unique User ID, Username, Role, and Contact Information. Each department has multiple users, and each user has a different Permission Level.",
  "input_json": "/data/RSchema_ExpResults4/Experiment2/schema2text_codellama13b/0167.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: rschema_0167\nCREATE TABLE `natural_disaster_data` (\n  `data_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `type` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `source` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `upload_time` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `content` text NOT NULL,\n  `format` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `layer_type` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`data_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `region_information` (\n  `region_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `administrative_division` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `latitude` decimal(9,6) NOT NULL,\n  `longitude` decimal(9,6) NOT NULL,\n  `area` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  `disaster_risk_level` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`region_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `department` (\n  `department_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `person_in_charge` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `contact_information` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `functional_scope` text NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`department_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `user` (\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `username` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `role` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `contact_information` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `permissions` text NOT NULL,\n  `department_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`user_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `user_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`department_id`) REFERENCES `department` (`department_id`) ON DELETE RESTRICT ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `region_and_department` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `department_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `division_scope` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `region_and_department_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`department_id`) REFERENCES `department` (`department_id`) ON DELETE RESTRICT ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
